**user**:
> How do I scale colors in ggplot according to a double-log-transformation? I have both positive and negative values that go from -.1 to -1e-6 and 1e6 to .1. I want to use something like a sigmoidal transform, so 0 is 0, but I have a scalable mechanism to say that that "fades" into a log transform for values more than X away from zero.
**assistant**:
> You’re looking for a **symmetric “pseudo-log” colour scale**:  
> - handles both positive and negative values,  
> - is **linear near 0** (so 0 is well-resolved),  
> - becomes **log-like farther out**,  
> - and has a tunable “distance from zero” where it starts to behave like log.
> 
> There is no built‑in ggplot2 scale that does this *and* handles negatives cleanly, but you can do it with a **custom transformation** or by using a standard “modulus transform” that already exists in the ecosystem.
> 
> Below are two practical routes:
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 1. Use a modulus (pseudo-log) transform from `scales` / `scico` (recommended quick route)
> 
> ### A. `modulus_trans` (scales) or `pseudo_log_trans` (scico)
> 
> The idea is to use a transform like:
> 
> \[
> T(x) = \operatorname{sign}(x)\,\frac{\log(1 + |x| / \lambda)}{\log(1 + 1/\lambda)}
> \]
> 
> - $\lambda$ is your tunable “**linear window half-width**” around 0.
> - For $|x| \ll \lambda$, $T(x) \approx x / (\lambda \log(1 + 1/\lambda))$ → **approximately linear**.
> - For $|x| \gg \lambda$, $T(x) \approx \operatorname{sign}(x)\left[\log(|x|) - \log(\lambda)\right]/\log(1+1/\lambda)$ → **log-like growth**.
> - Exactly symmetric in sign.
> 
> Some packages:
> 
> - `scales::modulus_trans(p)` is similar (Box–Cox–style);  
> - `scico::pseudo_log_trans(sigma)` does effectively this.
> 
> Example using `scico`:
> 
> ```r
> library(ggplot2)
> library(scico)    # for pseudo_log_trans
> library(scales)   # for label_number, etc.
> 
> sigma <- 0.01     # controls linear region width; tune to taste
> 
> ggplot(df, aes(x, y, fill = z)) +
>   geom_tile() +
>   scale_fill_gradient2(
>     trans = pseudo_log_trans(sigma = sigma),
>     low = "blue", mid = "white", high = "red",
>     midpoint = 0,
>     labels = label_number()
>   )
> ```
> 
> - Make `sigma` roughly the magnitude where you want to transition from linear to log‑like behaviour.  
> - For your ranges (from about $10^{-6}$ to $10^{-1}$ and $10^{-1}$ to $10^{6}$), you might try `sigma` in the $10^{-3}$–$10^{-1}$ range and see which gives a pleasing “fade”.
> 
> If you prefer `scales::modulus_trans`:
> 
> ```r
> library(ggplot2)
> library(scales)
> 
> p <- 0.1  # controls curvature; smaller = more linear near zero
> 
> mod_trans <- modulus_trans(p)
> 
> ggplot(df, aes(x, y, fill = z)) +
>   geom_tile() +
>   scale_fill_gradient2(
>     trans = mod_trans,
>     low = "blue", mid = "white", high = "red",
>     midpoint = 0,
>     labels = label_number()
>   )
> ```
> 
> This is not *exactly* the sigmoid you described, but it’s the same family of idea: symmetric, smooth, linear-ish near zero, log-like outwards, tunable with a single parameter.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 2. Define your own “symmetric soft-log” transform
> 
> If you want something closer to your verbal description (“0 is 0, fades into log for values more than X away from zero”), you can **hand-roll** a transform and plug it into `scale_fill_gradient2()`.
> 
> A common and smooth choice:
> 
> \[
> T(x; a) = \operatorname{sign}(x)\,\log\bigl(1 + |x| / a\bigr)
> \]
> 
> - $a > 0$ defines your “**fade distance**” around 0.
> - $T(0) = 0$; smooth and strictly monotone.
> - For $|x| \ll a$:
>   \[
>   T(x) \approx \operatorname{sign}(x)\frac{|x|}{a} = \frac{x}{a}
>   \]
>   → **approximately linear**.
> - For $|x| \gg a$:
>   \[
>   T(x) \approx \operatorname{sign}(x)\left[\log|x| - \log a\right]
>   \]
>   → **log‑like**.
> 
> Define it as a `scales::trans_new` object:
> 
> ```r
> library(ggplot2)
> library(scales)
> 
> # a is your "fade distance" from zero: for |x| << a, mapping is ~linear
> softlog_trans <- function(a = 0.01) {
>   trans <- function(x) {
>     sign(x) * log1p(abs(x) / a)
>   }
>   inv <- function(y) {
>     sign(y) * a * (exp(abs(y)) - 1)
>   }
>   # Optional: "nice" breaks in data space rather than transformed space
>   brks <- function(x) {
>     pretty(x)  # or something custom
>   }
>   trans_new(
>     name      = paste0("softlog-", a),
>     transform = trans,
>     inverse   = inv,
>     breaks    = brks
>   )
> }
> 
> # Example usage
> a <- 0.01
> 
> ggplot(df, aes(x, y, fill = z)) +
>   geom_tile() +
>   scale_fill_gradient2(
>     trans = softlog_trans(a),
>     low = "blue", mid = "white", high = "red",
>     midpoint = 0,
>     labels = label_number()
>   )
> ```
> 
> To choose `a`:
> 
> - pick the approximate $|z|$ where you want the mapping to “start feeling loggy”.  
> - For your domain, if you want:
>   - very fine resolution near 0 between about $10^{-4}$ and $10^{-2}$, maybe set `a = 1e-3` or `1e-2`;
>   - if you want 0 only gently favoured and most of the range treated more like log, choose smaller `a` (e.g. `1e-4`).
> 
> You can also make it *more sigmoidal* by using `\operatorname{asinh}` or a logistic-style transform, but the core interface into ggplot is the same: define `(transform, inverse)` and pass it to `trans`.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 3. Putting it together with your ranges
> 
> Given your ranges:
> 
> - negatives: roughly $[-10^{-1}, -10^{-6}]$  
> - positives: roughly $[10^{-6}, 10^{6}]$ (I’m assuming you meant $10^{-6}$ on the small positive side)
> 
> You likely want:
> 
> - strong resolution around $|z| \lesssim 10^{-3}$ or $10^{-2}$,  
> - values beyond that to compress log-like so the $10^6$ tail doesn’t dominate.
> 
> I’d try:
> 
> ```r
> ggplot(df, aes(x, y, fill = z)) +
>   geom_tile() +
>   scale_fill_gradient2(
>     trans = softlog_trans(a = 1e-3),   # tune: 1e-2, 1e-3, etc.
>     low = "blue", mid = "white", high = "red",
>     midpoint = 0,
>     limits = c(-1e6, 1e6),             # or computed from data
>     oob = squish,                      # to avoid warnings for out-of-range
>     labels = label_number()
>   )
> ```
> 
> ---
> 
> If you tell me the exact data range and which part you care about visually (e.g. “I want differences between ±0.001 clearly visible, but anything beyond ±100 can be heavily compressed”), I can propose specific parameter values (`sigma` or `a`) and a complete scale snippet tailored to that.
